WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:07.220
At the end of part 3, we saw that anyone can write `circle1.radius = -5`.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:09.509
Python doesn't complain.

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:15.730
Now the circle has a negative radius, and every area it computes is nonsense.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.600
How can a class protect its own data?

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:24.087
Welcome to Lecture 7, part 4: data hiding and encapsulation.

00:00:24.437 --> 00:00:29.120
We'll make data fields private, find out what Python really does with them,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.117
and then see why hiding the details makes a class easier to use.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:41.340
Letting code outside a class change its data fields directly is a bad idea, for two reasons.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.149
First, the data can be tampered with.

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:48.140
A circle gets a negative radius.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.850
A TV gets set to channel 500.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.413
Second, the class becomes hard to maintain.

00:00:54.413 --> 00:01:02.610
If the class later stores its data in a different way, every piece of code that touched the fields directly will break.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.532
Preventing direct access to the data fields is called data hiding.

00:01:07.532 --> 00:01:11.330
Other code works with the object only through its methods.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:19.325
In Python, a data field whose name starts with two underscores, like `__radius`, is private.

00:01:19.325 --> 00:01:22.780
It's meant to be used only inside the class.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:30.248
To let other code read or change a private field, the class provides a get method, called an accessor,

00:01:30.248 --> 00:01:32.690
and a set method, called a mutator.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.046
Here's the `Circle` class again, with a private radius.

00:01:37.046 --> 00:01:38.794
`get_radius` returns it.

00:01:38.794 --> 00:01:43.600
And `set_radius` only changes it if the new radius isn't negative.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.805
So `c = Circle(5)`, and `get_radius` gives 5.

00:01:48.805 --> 00:01:54.630
`set_radius(-3)` is ignored, so the radius is still 5.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:01.889
And if we try to read `c.__radius` from outside the class, we get an `AttributeError`.

00:02:01.889 --> 00:02:04.620
Python even suggests `get_radius`.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.008
The slides say private fields cannot be accessed outside the class.

00:02:10.008 --> 00:02:11.520
That's not quite true.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:19.885
Inside a class, Python quietly renames every name that starts with two underscores.

00:02:19.885 --> 00:02:25.390
`__radius`, in class `Circle`, becomes `_Circle__radius`.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:28.190
This is called name mangling.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.148
Outside the class, nothing is renamed.

00:02:32.148 --> 00:02:36.830
So `c.__radius` looks for a field that doesn't exist.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.559
But the field is still there, under its real name.

00:02:41.559 --> 00:02:47.338
The `vars` function shows an object's data fields as a dictionary.

00:02:47.338 --> 00:02:50.140
And `c._Circle__radius` gives 5.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:57.557
So a private name is a convention, and a warning: this is internal, don't touch it.

00:02:57.557 --> 00:02:59.005
It is not a lock.

00:02:59.005 --> 00:03:02.410
Python trusts programmers to respect it.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.469
Many Python programmers use a single underscore for the same message.

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:09.730
Python doesn't rename those at all.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.487
Pause the video and predict.

00:03:13.487 --> 00:03:21.807
We make `c = Circle(5)`, then write `c.__radius = 10`, from outside the class.

00:03:21.807 --> 00:03:25.860
What does `c.get_radius()` return now?

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:27.381
It returns 5.

00:03:27.381 --> 00:03:30.890
There's no error, and the radius didn't change.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:39.085
Outside the class, that line just added a new field to the object, called `__radius`.

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:41.669
`vars` shows two fields now.

00:03:41.669 --> 00:03:44.160
The real radius is still 5.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.520
If you want to change the radius, use `set_radius`.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.100
Three quick practice questions from the slides.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.285
1. Class `A` stores a private `i`.

00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:57.119
Then we print `a.__i`.

00:03:57.119 --> 00:03:59.370
That's an `AttributeError`.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:03.539
Outside the class, it isn't found under that name.

00:04:03.539 --> 00:04:07.290
Add a method, `get_i`, and call that instead.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:13.750
2. This class has a private `s`, and a method named `print`, which prints it.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.257
Is it correct? Yes.

00:04:15.257 --> 00:04:16.844
It prints 'Welcome'.

00:04:16.844 --> 00:04:22.874
Inside the method, the plain name `print` still means the built-in function.

00:04:22.874 --> 00:04:27.000
Still, a clearer name, like `show`, would be better.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:30.262
3. This class stores a private `on`,

00:04:30.262 --> 00:04:32.365
but the user reads `a.on`.

00:04:32.365 --> 00:04:37.217
There's no field called `on`, so that's an `AttributeError`.

00:04:37.217 --> 00:04:42.150
Fix it with a get method, `is_on`, which returns `True` here.

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:44.614
Now let's step back.

00:04:44.614 --> 00:04:51.334
Abstraction means separating the use of a piece of code from its implementation.

00:04:51.684 --> 00:04:54.317
You've done this since Lecture 4.

00:04:54.317 --> 00:05:03.334
Function abstraction separates what a function does, its name, parameters, and return value, from how it does it.

00:05:03.334 --> 00:05:07.324
You call `math.sqrt` without knowing how it works.

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:08.796
Here's `gcd`.

00:05:08.796 --> 00:05:13.632
Anyone can use it from its first line and its docstring.

00:05:13.632 --> 00:05:15.359
`gcd(24, 36)` is 12.

00:05:15.359 --> 00:05:22.354
If someone replaces the loop with a faster algorithm, every caller keeps working.

00:05:22.704 --> 00:05:26.276
Abstraction also lets two people work on one program.

00:05:26.276 --> 00:05:29.309
Programmer 1 writes and maintains `is_prime`.

00:05:29.309 --> 00:05:34.904
Programmer 2 writes `first_primes`, and only needs to know what `is_prime` returns.

00:05:35.254 --> 00:05:42.408
If everything were written together in one function, the prime test would be buried in the middle of the counting loop.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:45.534
Neither part could be tested, or reused, on its own.

00:05:46.334 --> 00:05:49.987
Class abstraction applies the same idea to a class.

00:05:49.987 --> 00:05:54.214
It separates how a class is implemented from how it's used.

00:05:54.564 --> 00:06:03.316
The class's methods, together with a description of what each one does, form the class's contract with its users.

00:06:03.316 --> 00:06:07.343
The user needs the contract, not the implementation.

00:06:07.343 --> 00:06:10.364
For the user, the class is a black box.

00:06:10.964 --> 00:06:13.976
Encapsulation is how a class does this.

00:06:13.976 --> 00:06:21.698
It puts the data, and the methods that work on it, together in one object, and it hides the details.

00:06:21.698 --> 00:06:23.861
Private fields are the tool.

00:06:23.861 --> 00:06:26.564
The contract is what the user sees.

00:06:27.334 --> 00:06:30.787
Here's an example: a Body Mass Index class.

00:06:30.787 --> 00:06:33.197
The contract is a UML diagram.

00:06:33.197 --> 00:06:37.374
A minus sign in front of a field means it's private.

00:06:37.724 --> 00:06:43.847
A BMI object has a name, an age, a weight in pounds, and a height in inches.

00:06:43.847 --> 00:06:48.924
Its methods return the BMI, the status, and each of the fields.

00:06:49.274 --> 00:06:53.780
From the contract alone, we can already use it.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:58.670
John Doe, 18 years old, 145 pounds, 70 inches tall.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:02.314
Peter King, 50, 215 pounds, 70 inches.

00:07:02.664 --> 00:07:09.700
The program prints: John Doe's BMI is 20.81, normal.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.624
Peter King's is 30.85, obese.

00:07:13.974 --> 00:07:15.729
Here's the implementation.

00:07:15.729 --> 00:07:17.889
The user never needs to read it.

00:07:17.889 --> 00:07:25.854
Notice that `get_status` calls another method of the same object, `self.get_BMI()`, rather than repeating the formula.

00:07:26.634 --> 00:07:27.293
One more.

00:07:27.293 --> 00:07:36.151
A `Loan` class has four private fields: the annual interest rate, the number of years, the loan amount, and the borrower,

00:07:36.151 --> 00:07:38.714
with a get and set method for each.

00:07:39.064 --> 00:07:45.478
A loan of 1000, over 5 years at 2.5%, for John Jones.

00:07:45.478 --> 00:07:53.104
The monthly payment is 17.75, and the total payment is 1064.84.

00:07:53.454 --> 00:08:00.006
The user of `Loan` knows what `get_monthly_payment` returns, but not the formula behind it.

00:08:00.006 --> 00:08:04.974
If the bank changes how it computes payments, only the class changes.

00:08:05.767 --> 00:08:07.047
Let's sum up.

00:08:07.397 --> 00:08:13.130
Private fields, with two leading underscores, are reached through get and set methods,

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:15.597
and a set method checks its argument.

00:08:15.947 --> 00:08:21.375
Python mangles a private name to `_ClassName__name`.

00:08:21.375 --> 00:08:24.507
It's a convention, not a lock.

00:08:24.857 --> 00:08:28.270
Abstraction separates use from implementation.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:34.577
Encapsulation bundles data with its methods, and hides the details behind a contract.

00:08:34.927 --> 00:08:36.204
Here's one to try.

00:08:36.204 --> 00:08:39.609
Write an `Account` class with a private balance.

00:08:39.609 --> 00:08:41.950
`deposit` adds a positive amount.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:46.419
`withdraw` takes money out only if the balance is large enough.

00:08:46.419 --> 00:08:49.967
Why is a private balance better than a public one?

00:08:50.517 --> 00:08:56.237
In Lecture 8, we'll build new classes on top of old ones, with inheritance.

